{# Host-aware web-UI exposure. Driven by `expose_mode` (host var; default 'traefik',
   'lan' on LAN-only hosts like daniel-pi). A per-container `expose_mode` key overrides
   the host default. Three modes:

     traefik — Traefik routes the UI via labels (Authelia-gated when use_authelia);
               no host port is published. (main server)
     lan     — no Traefik labels; the UI port is published bound to the host's LAN IP
               ({{ server_ip }}), reachable only on the local network. (the Pi)
     tunnel  — NEITHER labels nor published ports. (daniel-cloud, the Oracle node)
               cloudflared runs as a sibling on the same Docker network and maps each
               public hostname straight to a container by service name, so there is no
               Traefik to label for and nothing needs a host port. Deliberately not a
               variant of 'traefik': that mode's labels name `certresolver=cloudflare`,
               whose DNS-01 challenge needs a DNS-EDITING Cloudflare token on the host.
               A tunnel token is scoped to the tunnel, so the node never holds a
               credential that can rewrite the zone. Emitting inert Traefik labels there
               would also read as though something on the box were routing.

   Adding a mode: `web_ui_labels` matches 'traefik' explicitly rather than "not lan", so
   a new mode defaults to emitting NOTHING and has to opt in. That direction is the safe
   one — a mode that silently inherits Traefik labels would advertise routes no proxy
   serves.

   Invoke BOTH macros at COLUMN 0 on their own line (NOT indented — each bakes in the
   service-block indentation itself, and renders empty, one harmless blank line, in the
   other mode). The surrounding template lines keep their normal indent, e.g.:

       labels:
{{ web_ui_labels('8096') }}
         {{ kuma(container_item.name) }}

{{ web_ui_ports_block('8096') }}

   `web_ui_ports_block` emits a complete `ports:` block, so it is for services whose only
   published port is the UI (glances). wg-easy, which always publishes its
   WireGuard UDP port, appends its LAN UI port with an inline conditional instead.
#}
{% from 'traefik.yml.j2' import labels with context %}
{% macro web_ui_labels(internal_port=none) -%}
{% if (container_item.expose_mode | default(expose_mode | default('traefik'))) == 'traefik' %}
      {{ labels(
          container_item.hostname | default(container_item.name),
          (internal_port if internal_port is not none else container_item.port) | string,
          (container_item.networks | default([docker_network]))[0],
          container_item.use_authelia
        ) }}
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
{% macro web_ui_ports_block(internal_port) -%}
{% if (container_item.expose_mode | default(expose_mode | default('traefik'))) == 'lan' %}
    ports:
      - "{{ server_ip }}:{{ container_item.port }}:{{ internal_port }}/tcp"
{%- endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
